{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Four smaller defects found by the same sandbox validation that produced tickets\n`grapy-agronomic-sealion` (device-resolution guessing) and `unamused-cloudy-junebug` (missing\n/var/gerrit mount assertion). Grouped because each is small and they share a file.\n\n(Line numbers below are as of `25d1d683e55a`, the pre-fix `origin/main`, so they can be\nchecked rather than trusted.)\n\n## F3 (MEDIUM) - fstab entries accumulate on volume replacement\n\n`mount_ebs_volume` guards with `grep -q \"$uuid\" /etc/fstab`, which only checks for the NEW UUID.\nNothing removes a previous volume's line for the SAME mount point. The runbook's own\n\"restore onto a fresh volume\" path triggers it. PROVEN:\n\n```\n### T8  STALE fstab entry after a volume REPLACEMENT\n   old UUID=c814b8a1-...  new UUID=b1e23678-...\n     UUID=c814b8a1-dba9-4a59-b84c-0a6d6b6b38b2 /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch xfs defaults,nofail 0 2\n     UUID=b1e23678-982c-4a83-b6ab-d5d144c1f57f /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch xfs defaults,nofail 0 2\n  [DEFECT]  2 fstab entries for the SAME mount point; the stale one is a dangling nofail entry\n            that persists across reboots\n```\n\n`nofail` HIDES it, so which volume ends up at the mount point becomes fstab-order-dependent —\npermanently, and silently. That is a latent wrong-data-at-the-mount-point hazard, not merely\nuntidy.\n\n## F4 (LOW) - chmod runs AFTER the marker writes\n\nScript order is `touch` declaration -> `touch` proof -> `chmod 0700`\n(`user_data.sh:175-177`; `:129-131` as originally cited is a closing brace and a comment\nbanner inside `mount_ebs_volume`, not the marker block). The recorded\nintent (and the runbook) place `chmod` BEFORE the markers. Impact is small — root-owned, brief\n0755 window — but script and documented intent diverge, and the divergence is the kind that\nquietly becomes load-bearing.\n\n## F5 (LOW) - the two resolution branches return different string forms\n\n```\n   nvme-cli path returns the GLOB path: /dev/nvme2n1\n   by-id fallback returns the REALPATH: /dev/loop2\n```\nSame device, different string. Harmless today; it means log lines differ by which branch fired,\nand any future device COMPARISON would be branch-dependent.\n\n## F6 (LOW) - `gate_scratch_mount` is not actually single-sourced\n\n`infra/terraform/variables.tf` declares `variable \"gate_scratch_mount\"` at `:63`, and its\ndescription at `:66` states that four things must agree and that the variable is where the\nvalue is single-sourced. But `infra/compose/docker-compose.yml:268` HARDCODES\n`REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR: \"/var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch\"` rather than deriving it. Changing the terraform\ndefault would desync them — producing exactly the silent fallback the variable's own documentation\nwarns about.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 (F3) - Writing an fstab entry for a mount point REMOVES any pre-existing entry for\n      that same mount point that is not the current UUID. Proven with the replacement\n      scenario above: exactly one entry afterwards, and an unrelated `/` entry survives.\n- [ ] AC2 (F3) - The prior fstab is backed up before modification, so a bad edit is\n      recoverable from the host itself with no network.\n- [ ] AC3 (F4) - `chmod 0700` precedes both marker writes, so a marker is never created while\n      the mount point is still world-readable.\n- [ ] AC4 (F5) - Both device-resolution branches return the same canonical string for the same\n      device.\n- [ ] AC5 (F6) - Either the compose value is derived from the single source, or the\n      `variables.tf` claim is corrected to name the places that must be updated together.\n\n## Testing\n\nEvery criterion is proven by executing the shipped shell, not by reading it. The oracle\nextracts the functions from `user_data.sh` verbatim and re-homes only their absolute path\nroots, so the matching logic under test is the real thing.\n\n- `pytest tests/unit/test_user_data_device_resolution_d614.py` - the whole oracle.\n- AC1: `pytest -k test_replacing_a_volume_leaves_exactly_one_entry_for_the_mount_point`\n- AC2: `pytest -k test_the_previous_fstab_is_backed_up_before_it_is_rewritten`\n- AC3: `pytest -k test_the_markers_are_never_written_into_a_world_readable_mount_point`\n      (a stubbed `touch` records the mount point's permission bits as it finds them)\n- AC4: `pytest -k test_both_resolution_branches_return_the_same_string_for_the_same_device`\n- Defect-seeded, so each guard is proven load-bearing rather than incidental:\n  `pytest -k \"test_removing_the_stale_entry_drop_restores_the_accumulation or test_removing_the_chmod_lets_the_markers_land_in_a_world_readable_directory\"`\n- Shell gates: `python scripts/check_shellcheck.py` and\n  `python scripts/check_templatefile_escapes.py`.\n\nAC5 is DEFERRED rather than met; the reason is recorded in a comment on this ticket (the\nagreement is already pinned offline by `tests/unit/test_gate_scratch_volume_aa40.py`, and the\nonly real fix is a templating design decision that belongs in its own ticket).\n\n## Validation provenance\n\nAll findings executed in a real Ubuntu 24.04 aarch64 VM: real `losetup` loop devices, real\n`mkfs.xfs` (xfsprogs 6.6.0), real `mount -a`, real `blkid`/`mountpoint`, rendered template run\nverbatim under `unshare --mount --propagation private`. Only `nvme-cli` was stubbed, since loop\ndevices have no NVMe identify page. `shellcheck -s bash` on the rendered script is clean."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788597463114293001,"uuid":"553c6374-9d65-4fee-844a-169133a12d9c"}